# Jira

Call the Jira Cloud REST API as the connected user via the bundled helper.

{{ACTION_AVAILABILITY_SECTION}}

## Usage

    python .opencode/skills/jira/jira_api.py <command> [args]

Read commands auto-paginate and prune empty fields. The writes are
`create-issue`, `update-issue`, `comment`, `transition`, and `delete-issue`. Use
`--raw` to skip empty-field pruning; `python jira_api.py <command> -h` shows its
flags.

Every command except `sites` is scoped to an Atlassian site by its cloud id,
which is resolved automatically from the first accessible site. Target a
specific site with a global `--cloud-id CLOUD_ID` or `--site "<name or url
substring>"` before the subcommand.

### Accessible sites

```
python jira_api.py sites
```

Lists the Atlassian sites (Jira Cloud instances) the grant can reach, each with
the `id` (cloud id) used to scope every other call.

### The connected user

```
python jira_api.py me
```

### Projects

```
python jira_api.py projects [--limit N]
```

### Search issues with JQL

```
python jira_api.py search 'project = ENG AND status != Done' [--limit N]
```

The argument is a JQL query. Example clauses: `project = ENG`,
`assignee = currentUser()`, `status = "In Progress"`, `text ~ "onboarding"`.

### Read an issue by id or key

```
python jira_api.py issue ISSUE_KEY_OR_ID [--fields summary,description,status]
```

By default it expands `summary`, `description`, `status`, `assignee`,
`priority`, `issuetype`, and `labels`.

### List an issue's comments

```
python jira_api.py comments ISSUE_KEY [--limit N]
```

### Create an issue (write)

```
python jira_api.py create-issue --project KEY --summary "Title" \
  --description "Body text" [--type Task]
```

`--type` is the issue type name (default `Task`). `--description` takes plain
text; the helper wraps it into Atlassian Document Format (ADF).

### Update an issue (write)

```
python jira_api.py update-issue ISSUE_KEY --summary "New title" \
  [--description "New body"] [--labels bug,backend]
```

Only the fields you pass are changed. An update may require the issue's `edit`
metadata; the helper sends the fields directly and surfaces any API error.

### Comment on an issue (write)

```
python jira_api.py comment ISSUE_KEY "Looking into this"
```

### Transition an issue (write)

```
python jira_api.py transitions ISSUE_KEY
python jira_api.py transition ISSUE_KEY TRANSITION_ID
```

`transitions` lists the available transitions for the issue (each with its
`id` and target status); `transition` applies one by id.

### Delete an issue (write)

```
python jira_api.py delete-issue ISSUE_KEY
```

## Output

JSON on stdout. List commands return `{"ok": true, "<key>": [...], "count": N,
"truncated": bool}` (`truncated` means more results existed past `--limit`).
Failures return `{"ok": false, "status": <code>, "error": "<message>"}` and exit
non-zero — surface the error rather than retrying blindly.

## Notes

- Issue keys look like `PROJ-123`; numeric ids also work. Get them from
  `search` or `projects` (project keys) output.
- Jira Cloud's v3 API renders rich text as Atlassian Document Format (ADF). The
  helper accepts plain text for descriptions and comment bodies and wraps it in
  a single ADF paragraph (`{"type": "doc", "version": 1, ...}`).
- The connected user's token is injected by the egress gateway — send no
  credentials yourself.
- A 401 / 403 usually means the connected user lacks permission on the project
  or issue, or the app wasn't granted the needed scope — tell the user rather
  than retrying.
- This built-in covers Jira **Cloud** only. Jira Data Center / Server is
  self-hosted with different auth and is not supported here.
